# CMS Localization (Farsi)

Admin UI is **Farsi-first, RTL**. Public site strings are customer-specific later — do not mix site theme resources with admin resources.

## Stack

- Use `IStringLocalizer<T>` / `IViewLocalizer` for all user-visible text.
- Resource files: `Resources/{Name}.resx` (+ `.fa.resx` / `.fa-IR.resx`) or shared `Resources/AdminShared*.resx`.
- Marker class `T` must live in the **assembly root namespace** (e.g. `CMS.Modules.Blog.Web.BlogAdmin`), **not** in a `Resources` namespace — with `ResourcesPath = "Resources"`, a marker in `….Web.Resources` looks up `….Web.Resources.Resources.*` and falls back to English keys.
- Default culture: `fa-IR`. Support `fa` as fallback.

```csharp
builder.Services.Configure<RequestLocalizationOptions>(o =>
{
    var culture = new CultureInfo("fa-IR");
    o.DefaultRequestCulture = new RequestCulture(culture);
    o.SupportedCultures = new[] { culture };
    o.SupportedUICultures = new[] { culture };
});
```

## Keys & Copy

- Keys are **English identifiers**; values are Persian:

```
Save = ذخیره
Cancel = انصراف
NotFound = مورد یافت نشد
```

- Prefer short, clear Persian. Avoid literal machine-translated tone.
- Use Persian digits in UI copy where natural; keep Latin for IDs, codes, emails, URLs.

## In Views & Code

```cshtml
@inject IViewLocalizer Localizer
<button>@Localizer["Save"]</button>
```

```csharp
_localizer["ProductCreated"]
```

- Validation messages (FluentValidation / DataAnnotations) must resolve to Persian resources.
- Never hardcode Persian or English UI strings in controllers for user-facing messages — use localizer keys.

## Do Not

- Concatenate sentences in code for grammar-sensitive Persian; use full resource strings with placeholders: `"{0} ذخیره شد"`.
- Put public-website marketing copy in Admin resource files.
- Assume LTR date/number formatting — use `fa-IR` culture formatting.
